preConditionHook:
// THE COUNT THAT FAILED, TAKEN AS AN EXIT. Brooks: "if the market reverses and
// trades below the low of the pattern before reaching at least a scalper's
// profit, the high 2 buy has failed." So a long is closed the moment price
// trades back below the COUNT'S SIGNAL BAR LOW while the trade is still short of
// scalpProfitTicks of profit; the short mirrors it above the signal bar's high.
//
// AN EXIT, NOT AN ENTRY, and that is why it cannot be reached by placing
// FailedCountReversal in the exit pane -- an Entry cannot sit there, the same
// call the catalog makes for ConsecutiveCloseExit. FailedCountReversal is the
// same EVENT put to the opposite use: it takes the other side, this one just
// gets out, and it carries a countTarget and a bars-since window that this rule
// does not. Not StopLoss, whose level is a fixed dollar loss rather than a
// PATTERN price, and not OpposingCountExit, which reads the OTHER side's
// counter reaching a target rather than this side's signal bar failing.
//
// THE COUNTER IS THE SETTLED ONE of Ranges ch17 (R17-01), inlined verbatim as
// every leg-counting rule in this corpus does rather than referencing
// HighLowBarCount: a bar whose high exceeds the PRIOR bar's high advances the
// count, but only once a new low has formed since the last counted bar; the
// count RESETS to 0 when the close makes a fresh resetLookback-bar high; it
// SATURATES at 4. The reset window is Highest(high, resetLookback)[1], the bars
// ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so the current bar's own high cannot defeat its own reset.
// Highest / Lowest bottom out in EL's Extremes(), which is EL SOURCE and carries
// the relational tolerance, hence el_gt / el_lt in the scan.
//
// NO countTarget INPUT. R17-12 is about a failed count of ANY number -- and its
// own note, that a failed high 2 usually becomes a high 3, is simply the counter
// continuing to advance -- so the level tested is whatever signal bar the
// counter last produced, at any count. The merged parameters name no target and
// none is invented.
//
// THE HAVE-FLAGS are the one addition to the settled block: signalLow and
// signalHigh start at 0 like every EL variable, and on positive prices a 0 level
// can never be traded through, but a back-adjusted continuous contract can price
// below zero, so the flags say explicitly that a signal bar has been counted --
// the same guard FailedCountReversal adds for the same reason. They are MARKET
// state, not position state, so they are never reset at entry.
//
// OpenPositionProfit is NET of the entry side's costs (VERIFIED), which is what
// every threshold in this corpus measures, and BigPointValue converts the tick
// target to the same currency. The MarketPosition guard is what makes the rule
// an exit; the counter runs every bar whether or not a position is open.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
priorHigh = high[1];
priorLow = low[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)resetLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], priorHigh)) priorHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], priorLow)) priorLow = low[i];
}
if (el_gt(close[0], priorHigh)) {
    highCount = 0;
} else {
    if (el_lt(low[0], minLowSince)) minLowSince = low[0];
    if (el_gt(high[0], high[1]) && (highCount == 0 || el_lt(minLowSince, signalLow))) {
        highCount = highCount + 1;
        if (highCount > 4) highCount = 4;
        signalLow = low[0];
        minLowSince = low[0];
        haveSigLow = true;
    }
}
if (el_lt(close[0], priorLow)) {
    lowCount = 0;
} else {
    if (el_gt(high[0], maxHighSince)) maxHighSince = high[0];
    if (el_lt(low[0], low[1]) && (lowCount == 0 || el_gt(maxHighSince, signalHigh))) {
        lowCount = lowCount + 1;
        if (lowCount > 4) lowCount = 4;
        signalHigh = high[0];
        maxHighSince = high[0];
        haveSigHigh = true;
    }
}
